</span></div>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Architecture ablation.<span id="S4.T1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We ablate the main components of our architecture: the token reduction module, the interleave factor, and the distilled residual tokenizer. We set the token reduction probability <math id="S4.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and use the ViT-L/14 backbone. </span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">With an average frame encoding cost of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="233.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>233.4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">233.4</annotation></semantics></math> GFLOPs, the CLIP baseline (<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a.</span> in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) establishes our upper bound target grounding accuracy, which we wish to maintain but with much higher computational efficiency.
When we apply token reduction across all frames (<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b.</span>), we observe an <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="85\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>85</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">85\%</annotation></semantics></math> decrease in computational cost. However, this setting induces marked absolute declines in grounding accuracy of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> in our metrics, which translates to a relative drop of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="34{-}40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>34</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">34{-}40\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
The introduction of our interleave strategy (<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">c.</span>), which alternates encoding one frame without token reduction and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> frames with token reduction (where <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>), shows an increase in grounding accuracy of <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.4\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10.4</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.4\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.3\%</annotation></semantics></math> while only using <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the original computational budget, which is a first step in closing the grounding accuracy gap with respect to the target CLIP accuracy. Compared to the full CLIP model, the grounding accuracy drop narrows to a modest <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.3</annotation></semantics></math> percentage points (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5{-}9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5{-}9\%</annotation></semantics></math> relative drop), yet this configuration only incurs <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the original computational cost. Further, adding the residual tokenizer learned via distillation (<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">d.</span>) comes at a negligible compute cost but further boosts grounding accuracy closer to the target CLIP model, showing only a minor <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\sim}1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\sim}1\%</annotation></semantics></math> absolute drop.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13255v1/05-N_ablation_motion_p_drop_0.85.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/363;" width="685" height="363" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Interleaving frames (<math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.F4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. Our ResidualViT (</span><span id="S4.F4.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">orange</span><span id="S4.F4.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">) closely retains CLIP’s (</span><span id="S4.F4.7.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span><span id="S4.F4.7.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">) performance for <math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> while reducing cost by <math id="S4.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interleave Factor <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> and Benefits of Distillation.</span> In Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we explore the relationship between grounding accuracy and computational cost as we vary the number of interleaved frames (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>).
Here, the baseline CLIP model is shown in <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">red</span>, while our ResidualViT, applied with and without the distilled residual tokenizer module, is shown in <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF8000;">orange</span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#3B76AF;">blue</span>, respectively. We vary <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{1,2,3,5,10\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{1,2,3,5,10\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When setting <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}1</annotation></semantics></math>, grounding accuracy is marginally impacted, yet a large computational cost reduction is already achieved (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="42\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>42</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">42\%</annotation></semantics></math>). Notably, we see a further accuracy drop when the residual tokenizer is removed (<span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#3B76AF;">blue</span>), demonstrating the importance of the distillation training. At <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>, the cost savings increases to <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56\%</annotation></semantics></math> with virtually no accuracy change for ResidualViT. However, the importance of the learnable residual connection (via the residual token learnt by the distillation training) becomes more evident as the difference between the two configurations widens with substantial drops when the residual token is not employed. Increasing <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> beyond this point (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2</annotation></semantics></math>) sees diminishing returns in cost savings, now at <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="63\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>63</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63\%</annotation></semantics></math>, and a noticeable decrease in accuracy. This decline is attributed to the growing temporal gap between I-features and P-features, leading to a weakened visual correlation and, thus, reduced efficacy. We regard <math id="S4.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}2</annotation></semantics></math> as the best trade-off.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Benchmarking on Downstream Tasks</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we compare the quality vs. runtime trade-offs achieved by ResidualViT with respect to CLIP. We do so on three benchmarks for the multi-modal Natural Language Temporal Video grounding task, comprising two short-form video datasets (Charades-STA and ActivityNet-Captions) and one long-form video dataset (MAD). Additionally, we also explore ResidualViT’s generalization capabilities by applying it to the complementary task of Automatic Audio Description generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, Temporal Activity Localization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and Action Recognition (see Section <a href="#S11" title="11 Additional Task: Action Recognition ‣ ResidualViT for Efficient Temporally Dense Video Encoding" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> of supplementary). In all experiments, unless specified, we use ResidualViT (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{=}85\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>85</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{=}85\%</annotation></semantics></math>), which yields a cost saving of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="53\%-56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>53</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>56</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">53\%-56\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T2.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:496.9pt;height:144.1pt;vertical-align:-69.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(6.7pt,-1.9pt) scale(1.02757045105908,1.02757045105908) ;">
